 "verdicts": {
  "Agents were told to implement one feature at a time because attempting the whole application exhausted context mid-implementation and left half-finished work.": 1,
  "Anthropic developed a two-part solution for the Claude Agent SDK to work across many context windows: an initializer agent that sets up the environment on the first run, and a coding agent that makes incremental progress in every session while leaving clear artifacts for the next session.": 1,
  "Anthropic found the best way to get the model to leave the environment in a clean state was to ask it to commit progress to git with descriptive commit messages and write progress summaries in a progress file.": 1,
  "Anthropic recommends a two-stage harness for work spanning context windows: an initializer agent runs only in the first session to prepare the environment, and coding agents run in later sessions making incremental progress and leaving artifacts for the next session.": 1,
  "Anthropic reports compaction alone was insufficient in the original long-running experiment: an Opus 4.5 loop could attempt too much, exhaust context mid-implementation leaving undocumented partial work, and later declare the project done prematurely.": 1,
  "At session start, the coding agent runs pwd, reads the git logs and progress files, and reads the feature list file to choose the highest-priority feature not yet done.": 1,
  "At the start of each session the coding agent inspects its working directory, reads progress notes, recent Git history and the feature list, starts the app via init.sh, runs a basic end-to-end smoke test, and picks the highest-priority unfinished feature; at the end it commits and writes a progress update.": 1,
  "Claude tended to mark features complete without end-to-end testing until explicitly prompted to use browser automation (Puppeteer MCP); a limitation is that Claude can't see browser-native alert modals through Puppeteer MCP.": 1,
  "Coding agents are told to edit the feature-list JSON only by changing a passes field and not to remove or rewrite tests; Anthropic found JSON less likely than Markdown to be inappropriately overwritten.": 1,
  "Coding agents work on one feature at a time, commit to git with descriptive messages, and write progress summaries, which lets them revert bad changes and recover working states.": 1,
  "Compaction alone isn't sufficient: even Opus 4.5 on the Claude Agent SDK looping across context windows failed to build a production-quality claude.ai clone from a high-level prompt, and compaction doesn't always pass clear instructions to the next agent.": 1,
  "Each coding agent session is asked to work on only one feature at a time.": 1,
  "Each coding session begins by checking the working directory, reading progress notes, git history and the feature list, starting the dev server and running a basic end-to-end test, and ends with a descriptive commit and progress update.": 1,
  "Each coding session starts by running pwd, reading git logs and progress files, reading the feature list to choose the highest-priority unfinished feature, and running init.sh plus a basic end-to-end test before new work.": 1,
  "In the Claude.ai-clone example, the initializer produced more than 200 features as a JSON list initially marked failing; coding agents were instructed to change only the passes status, because models were less likely to inappropriately overwrite JSON than Markdown.": 1,
  "In the long-running harness design, an initializer session creates init.sh, claude-progress.txt and an initial git commit, and subsequent coding agents make incremental progress and leave structured handoffs.": 1,
  "Long-running agents work in discrete sessions with no memory of prior sessions; Anthropic's solution for the Claude Agent SDK is an initializer agent that sets up the environment on the first run and a coding agent that makes incremental progress each session and leaves clear artifacts.": 1,
  "The coding agent in every subsequent session is asked to make incremental progress and then leave structured updates.": 1,
  "The initializer agent is instructed to create an init.sh setup script, a claude-progress.txt work log, and an initial Git commit showing what files were added.": 1,
  "The initializer agent writes an init.sh script, a claude-progress.txt log of what agents have done, and an initial git commit showing the files added.": 1,
  "The initializer agent's first session uses a specialized prompt to set up an init.sh script, a claude-progress.txt file that logs what agents have done, and an initial git commit showing which files were added.": 1,
  "The initializer expands the prompt into a comprehensive end-to-end feature list; Anthropic's Claude.ai-clone example had more than 200 features, initially marked failing.": 1,
  "The initializer wrote a feature list of over 200 features in JSON, all initially marked failing with a passes field; coding agents may only change passes, and JSON was chosen because the model is less likely to inappropriately overwrite it than Markdown.": 1,
  "To prevent the agent from one-shotting an app or prematurely considering the project complete, the initializer agent is prompted to write a comprehensive file of feature requirements expanding on the user's initial prompt.": 1,
  "Two observed failure patterns: the agent tried to one-shot the app and ran out of context mid-feature, and a later agent saw existing progress and declared the job done.": 1,
  "Unit tests and curl checks were insufficient to detect broken user workflows; explicit instructions to use browser automation via Puppeteer MCP substantially improved failure detection.": 1,
